{"id":"dc26d1a0a1d3f8bf03c9a62c","source":"guardian-rss","source_name":"The Guardian World","platform":"rss","url":"https://www.theguardian.com/world/2026/aug/25/colombia-immigration-crackdown-president-abelardo-de-la-espriella","published_at":"2026-08-25T00:34:47Z","collected_at":"2026-08-25T01:48:34.513319Z","title":"Colombia’s new president Abelardo de la Espriella announces immigration crackdown","text":"Colombian president Abelardo de la Espriella says the operations against ‘illegal immigrants’ will involve police and the migration authority. Photograph: Pablo R Seco/EPA View image in fullscreen Colombian president Abelardo de la Espriella says the operations against ‘illegal immigrants’ will involve police and the migration authority. Photograph: Pablo R Seco/EPA Colombia Colombia’s new president Abelardo de la Espriella announces immigration crackdown Far-right leader echoes ally Donald Trump and says operations to ‘track down illegal immigrants’ should begin this week, amid earthquake aftermath Tiago Rogero South America correspondent Mon 24 Aug 2026 20.34 EDT Last modified on Mon 24 Aug 2026 21.15 EDT Share Prefer the Guardian on Google Colombia’s far-right president, Abelardo de la Espriella, has announced a crackdown on immigrants, ordering authorities to detain and deport people who are in the country irregularly or have committed crimes. In office for just over two weeks, the millionaire former lawyer made the announcement on Sunday as the country continued to grapple with the aftermath of a 7.4-magnitude earthquake on 10 August that killed more than 300 people and destro","html":"","media":["https://i.guim.co.uk/img/media/16d4042f8c9b2aabb47619aa73c090daec510abd/784_32_3286_2630/master/3286.jpg?width=465&dpr=1&s=none&crop=none"],"author":"Tiago Rogero South America correspondent","language":"und","group":"international-media","perspective":"mixed","trust":"high","tags":[],"raw":{"archive_policy":"public_excerpt_v1","content_sha256":"1f7c0111b125d1bba672f3e5438b2f4b5ba2e78a3eae36ad38fc0d19116ca1ff","original_text_chars":4406,"original_html_chars":18283,"text_truncated":true,"media_count":1,"platform":{"entry_id":"https://www.theguardian.com/world/2026/aug/25/colombia-immigration-crackdown-president-abelardo-de-la-espriella","entry_link":"https://www.theguardian.com/world/2026/aug/25/colombia-immigration-crackdown-president-abelardo-de-la-espriella"}}}
{"id":"30340239b6b712f254433663","source":"guardian-rss","source_name":"The Guardian World","platform":"rss","url":"https://www.theguardian.com/world/2026/aug/25/japan-1800-people-move-pull-400-tonne-hirosaki-castle-with-ropes","published_at":"2026-08-25T01:10:44Z","collected_at":"2026-08-25T01:48:49.741229Z","title":"Japan enlists 1,800 people to drag a 360-tonne castle keep using ropes and rollers","text":"People pull on ropes attached to a platform carrying the Hirosaki Castle keep in north-eastern Japan at the weekend. ‘It was fantastic,’ one participant says. Photograph: Kyodo News/Getty Images View image in fullscreen People pull on ropes attached to a platform carrying the Hirosaki Castle keep in north-eastern Japan at the weekend. ‘It was fantastic,’ one participant says. Photograph: Kyodo News/Getty Images Japan Japan enlists 1,800 people to drag a 360-tonne castle keep using ropes and rollers After repairs to Hirosaki Castle in Aomori prefecture, officials employed a technique known as hikiya to move the structure more than two metres Gavin Blair in Tokyo Mon 24 Aug 2026 21.10 EDT Last modified on Mon 24 Aug 2026 21.11 EDT Share Prefer the Guardian on Google To chants of “so-re, so-re”, about 1,800 people came together in northern Japan over the weekend to pull a 360-tonne castle keep a little more than two metres closer to its original resting place as part of ongoing renovations. The keep – a fortified tower within a castle – had to be moved to repair a wall that was damaged in a major earthquake. Rotating teams of 80 people heaved on 30-metre ropes as the keep of Hirosaki ","html":"","media":["https://i.guim.co.uk/img/media/1402f53e1dbf0a1c2705eb53dcbb04b16ee4f1ee/0_0_3968_2645/master/3968.jpg?width=465&dpr=1&s=none&crop=none","https://i.guim.co.uk/img/uploads/2026/08/25/About_1_800_people_pull_400-tonne_Hirosaki_castle_in_Aomori_prefecture--6c205d81-6d97-4f39-9010-3e384f3a2e0e-3.0.0000000.jpg?width=120&dpr=1&s=none&crop=5%3A4","https://i.guim.co.uk/img/media/51e9e739699ed51dca529e2169dcfbb52485b0df/0_0_5861_3900/master/5861.jpg?width=445&dpr=1&s=none&crop=none"],"author":"Gavin Blair in Tokyo","language":"und","group":"international-media","perspective":"mixed","trust":"high","tags":[],"raw":{"archive_policy":"public_excerpt_v1","content_sha256":"561a87b69d0516e320bbbb4d1de8d68241a8900c8ea95ad06cee64ae5d08eb55","original_text_chars":4290,"original_html_chars":23032,"text_truncated":true,"media_count":3,"platform":{"entry_id":"https://www.theguardian.com/world/2026/aug/25/japan-1800-people-move-pull-400-tonne-hirosaki-castle-with-ropes","entry_link":"https://www.theguardian.com/world/2026/aug/25/japan-1800-people-move-pull-400-tonne-hirosaki-castle-with-ropes"}}}
{"id":"7ccf988ccf6b61a013b49002","source":"guardian-rss","source_name":"The Guardian World","platform":"rss","url":"https://www.theguardian.com/australia-news/live/2026/aug/25/australia-news-live-sydney-trains-delays-cancellations-albanese-gst-wa-one-nation-coalition-secret-harbour-election-tax-reform-royal-commission-antisemitism-icac-victorian-rail-loop-alan-jones-trial-ntwnfb","published_at":"2026-08-25T01:35:24Z","collected_at":"2026-08-25T01:49:06.691203Z","title":"Australia news live: Coles blames competitor’s Ooshies for slowing sales; young people the first generation to earn less than their parents at same age","text":"19.36 EDT Coles blames Ooshies for hurting its sales as it reports $1.09bn profit Luca Ittimani Coles reported growing profit from its supermarkets despite a slump in alcohol sales and a delayed hit from Woolworths’ Disney “Ooshie” giveaways. The company today said it grown supermarket sales by 3.7% and gained market share over the year to June. Growth had continued into July but slumped amid a “competitor’s collectibles campaign” – a reference to the “Ooshie” plastic figurines. Coles said its digital sales boomed 26.4%, to $5.6bn, helped by a partnership with Uber Eats and improved wait times for store-based click and collect orders. Its own-brand product sales grew 6.1%, with the company singling out its cleaning, baby and ready meal range. The company said the prices of its supermarket products rose just 1.5% through the year. Supermarkets’ own calculation methods for inflation are not necessarily intuitive. Its net profit bumped up 1% to $1.09bn as a result. Profit would have been $1.25bn if not for a penalty from a federal court ruling over its payment of managers . But liquor sales fell 3.3% from the prior year. The company said “subdued consumer sentiment” and cost of living","html":"","media":["https://i.guim.co.uk/img/uploads/2025/04/30/Luca_Ittimani.png?width=300&quality=85&auto=format&fit=max&s=b269ad3cfa5a0e929b1c0afc69304369"],"author":"Nick Visser","language":"und","group":"international-media","perspective":"mixed","trust":"high","tags":[],"raw":{"archive_policy":"public_excerpt_v1","content_sha256":"38a3082e8178191a4fe10b424c1c241f3a6aa610d11a838f6a94129e64b0f6f5","original_text_chars":1332,"original_html_chars":2484,"text_truncated":true,"media_count":1,"platform":{"entry_id":"https://www.theguardian.com/australia-news/live/2026/aug/25/australia-news-live-sydney-trains-delays-cancellations-albanese-gst-wa-one-nation-coalition-secret-harbour-election-tax-reform-royal-commission-antisemitism-icac-victorian-rail-loop-alan-jones-trial-ntwnfb","entry_link":"https://www.theguardian.com/australia-news/live/2026/aug/25/australia-news-live-sydney-trains-delays-cancellations-albanese-gst-wa-one-nation-coalition-secret-harbour-election-tax-reform-royal-commission-antisemitism-icac-victorian-rail-loop-alan-jones-trial-ntwnfb"}}}
{"id":"b475526cec42af08185ac18b","source":"guardian-rss","source_name":"The Guardian World","platform":"rss","url":"https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2026/aug/24/hawk-fire-reno-nevada-injuries-evacuations","published_at":"2026-08-25T01:28:27Z","collected_at":"2026-08-25T01:49:18.504502Z","title":"Fast-moving Hawk wildfire is about 27% contained in Reno, Nevada, officials say","text":"The Hawk fire burns on 23 August 2026 in Reno, Nevada. Photograph: Trevor Bexon/Getty Images View image in fullscreen The Hawk fire burns on 23 August 2026 in Reno, Nevada. Photograph: Trevor Bexon/Getty Images Nevada Fast-moving Hawk wildfire is about 27% contained in Reno, Nevada, officials say Tens of thousands are still under evacuation orders or warnings amid ‘human-caused’, wind-driven blaze Edward Helmore , Dani Anguiano , Cecilia Nowell and agencies Mon 24 Aug 2026 13.26 EDT First published on Mon 24 Aug 2026 09.17 EDT Share Prefer the Guardian on Google The Hawk fire is about 27% contained, authorities said on Monday afternoon, two days after the fast-moving , wind-driven wildfire sparked near Reno, Nevada . Authorities added that 23,000 people are under a “go-now” order and 40,000 people are under a “get-set” warning, an improvement from the 90,000 Nevadans under evacuation orders or warnings on Monday morning. More than 900 personnel are currently assigned to the fire, said Richard Edwards, the chief of Truckee Meadows Fire Protection District, who added that first responders had identified 32 destroyed and six damaged homes. “We expect those numbers to increase as damag","html":"","media":["https://i.guim.co.uk/img/media/1eac02ef76fd62ba21d03ef83537c4d25956ca61/0_0_5512_3674/master/5512.jpg?width=465&dpr=1&s=none&crop=none","https://i.guim.co.uk/img/media/5b8530a5ecccbc524b922394f576441915ed27c1/0_315_1439_809/1439.jpg?width=465&dpr=1&s=none&crop=none","https://i.guim.co.uk/img/media/2892c6e08c79ce9fad169313d776e273b0f6a784/0_0_3454_1946/master/3454.jpg?width=445&dpr=1&s=none&crop=none"],"author":"Edward Helmore, Dani Anguiano , Cecilia Nowell and agencies","language":"und","group":"international-media","perspective":"mixed","trust":"high","tags":[],"raw":{"archive_policy":"public_excerpt_v1","content_sha256":"03a2e9dab38c03f6031c21dbc8618773a5435dee6e739348068222114395b59e","original_text_chars":6289,"original_html_chars":28528,"text_truncated":true,"media_count":3,"platform":{"entry_id":"https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2026/aug/24/hawk-fire-reno-nevada-injuries-evacuations","entry_link":"https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/2026/aug/24/hawk-fire-reno-nevada-injuries-evacuations"}}}
{"id":"84708fa93fb078fd1dd936cf","source":"guardian-rss","source_name":"The Guardian World","platform":"rss","url":"https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/live/2026/aug/24/hakeem-jeffries-jared-kushner-democrats-midterm-elections-donald-trump-iran-war-sanctions-latest-news-updates","published_at":"2026-08-25T01:18:48Z","collected_at":"2026-08-25T01:49:18.907036Z","title":"Gavin Newsom says California will sue Trump administration again to block ‘Orwellian rules’ meant to curb mail-in voting – live","text":"19.17 EDT California plans to sue again to stop Trump from restricting mail voting and could make blocking delivery a felony California’s governor, Gavin Newsom, said on Monday that his state would sue the Trump administration again to block federal changes to mail voting, after the US supreme court ruled that a previous suit was filed too early in the process by his state and 22 others. “California will be suing AGAIN to block these Orwellian rules from being implemented,” Newsom posted . Newsom also highlighted two new proposed state laws that would make it a crime to interfere with the delivery of mail ballots. In a press release, Newsom added that he and the California legislature “are doubling down to ensure California continues to defend democracy as new threats from Donald Trump and his election-rigging allies emerge”. Two draft laws would establish three new felonies under California state law, punishable by up to four years in prison. One of the laws would prohibit anyone “from seizing or causing the seizure of ballots, election records, or certified voting technology before election results are certified”, and blocks “any person with authority from directing someone in th","html":"","media":[],"author":"Robert Mackey (now); Anna Betts, Shrai Popat and Tom Ambrose (earlier)","language":"und","group":"international-media","perspective":"mixed","trust":"high","tags":[],"raw":{"archive_policy":"public_excerpt_v1","content_sha256":"b18f8735115c8c93785c03957612174b76a9719765505f4c9bc960c17c4b585e","original_text_chars":1548,"original_html_chars":2406,"text_truncated":true,"media_count":0,"platform":{"entry_id":"https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/live/2026/aug/24/hakeem-jeffries-jared-kushner-democrats-midterm-elections-donald-trump-iran-war-sanctions-latest-news-updates","entry_link":"https://www.theguardian.com/us-news/live/2026/aug/24/hakeem-jeffries-jared-kushner-democrats-midterm-elections-donald-trump-iran-war-sanctions-latest-news-updates"}}}
{"id":"83070643e6d51bda8e327d25","source":"guardian-rss","source_name":"The Guardian World","platform":"rss","url":"https://www.theguardian.com/business/2026/aug/24/washington-post-reinstate-karen-attiah","published_at":"2026-08-25T00:51:59Z","collected_at":"2026-08-25T01:49:19.190757Z","title":"Washington Post ordered to reinstate opinion writer fired over Charlie Kirk posts","text":"Karen Attiah leads a discussion at the Oslo Freedom Forum on 28 May 2019 in Oslo, Norway. Photograph: Julia Reinhart/Getty Images View image in fullscreen Karen Attiah leads a discussion at the Oslo Freedom Forum on 28 May 2019 in Oslo, Norway. Photograph: Julia Reinhart/Getty Images Business Washington Post ordered to reinstate opinion writer fired over Charlie Kirk posts Arbitrator determined the Post improperly fired Karen Attiah, a union-protected employee, over social media posts Jeremy Barr Mon 24 Aug 2026 20.51 EDT First published on Mon 24 Aug 2026 11.42 EDT Share Prefer the Guardian on Google An independent arbitrator has ordered the Washington Post to rehire the high-profile opinion columnist Karen Attiah, abruptly fired by the news organization last September over comments she made on social media about Charlie Kirk , the then recently killed conservative activist. On Monday, an arbitrator ruled that the Post had violated her rights as a union-protected employee and ordered the newspaper to reinstate her and compensate her for lost wages. “This decision confirms what we’ve said from the start: I was doing my job as an opinion journalist, and doing that job is not miscond","html":"","media":["https://i.guim.co.uk/img/media/4a8f4519a46a3097a4067b6c640c48a65a5f9639/499_0_5001_4000/master/5001.jpg?width=465&dpr=1&s=none&crop=none","https://media.guim.co.uk/e3d43299e85f27e579437406d4cbfb596d9d0c94/0_0_3000_3000/500.jpg"],"author":"Jeremy Barr","language":"und","group":"international-media","perspective":"mixed","trust":"high","tags":[],"raw":{"archive_policy":"public_excerpt_v1","content_sha256":"c58b6c904cd8c6f3f6adf15d4c1dbf14d6bc321dbcaab94aefcdc0c8b3d8c4af","original_text_chars":5365,"original_html_chars":20327,"text_truncated":true,"media_count":2,"platform":{"entry_id":"https://www.theguardian.com/business/2026/aug/24/washington-post-reinstate-karen-attiah","entry_link":"https://www.theguardian.com/business/2026/aug/24/washington-post-reinstate-karen-attiah"}}}
